1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,400
A mock is useful when its boundary matches the question you want to answer. This

2
00:00:05,412 --> 00:00:10,425
episode tests how the transfer page reacts to a temporary service error, then compares

3
00:00:10,438 --> 00:00:15,525
that evidence with the integrated cases already built. We will intercept one browser

4
00:00:15,538 --> 00:00:20,812
request and return a controlled response. That gives repeatable UI behavior without

5
00:00:20,825 --> 00:00:26,075
stopping services. It does not test whether the real backend generates that response

6
00:00:26,088 --> 00:00:30,912
during an outage. Write the scope beside the test name before implementing the route

7
00:00:30,925 --> 00:00:31,500
handler.

8
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:37,988
The local error scenario uses status five hundred three with a service unavailable

9
00:00:38,001 --> 00:00:43,064
code and a documented Spanish message. The page should display that message in an

10
00:00:43,076 --> 00:00:47,964
alert and make the transfer button usable again. Because the browser interception

11
00:00:47,976 --> 00:00:51,414
prevents this submission from reaching the backend, the account should remain

12
00:00:51,426 --> 00:00:57,814
unchanged. These expectations describe our specific experiment. They do not describe

13
00:00:57,826 --> 00:01:02,326
every possible service failure; a real timeout after a committed debit requires a

14
00:01:02,338 --> 00:01:05,614
different test and a careful idempotency recovery design.

15
00:01:07,832 --> 00:01:12,457
Create a fresh fixture, authenticate its context, and select the source account and

16
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:17,944
beneficiary. Confirm the opening balance before installing the response substitution.

17
00:01:18,807 --> 00:01:23,857
This separates setup failures from the error behavior under test. If your handler

18
00:01:23,870 --> 00:01:28,532
accidentally intercepts fixture creation or login, you are testing a different path.

19
00:01:29,395 --> 00:01:34,444
Keep the route match narrow enough to affect only transfer submission. The test should

20
00:01:34,457 --> 00:01:38,544
be able to explain exactly which request is controlled and which requests still

21
00:01:38,557 --> 00:01:40,895
communicate with the running local services.

22
00:01:43,114 --> 00:01:48,152
Register the handler before clicking. Match the transfer route and check the request

23
00:01:48,164 --> 00:01:53,564
method inside the handler. Requests using other methods continue to the real server.

24
00:01:54,426 --> 00:02:00,214
For the POST, fulfill a JSON response with the agreed status, content type, code, and

25
00:02:00,226 --> 00:02:06,526
message. The excerpt uses an ordinary JavaScript object converted to JSON, so the

26
00:02:06,539 --> 00:02:12,239
browser receives the intended structure. A broad wildcard that replaces every request

27
00:02:12,252 --> 00:02:16,976
can make a page appear resilient while hiding broken account queries, authentication,

28
00:02:17,239 --> 00:02:18,976
or unrelated loading behavior.

29
00:02:21,204 --> 00:02:26,692
Enter one hundred pesos and click the transfer button once. Wait for the exact alert

30
00:02:26,704 --> 00:02:32,054
through a locator assertion, then verify that the button is enabled again. The visible

31
00:02:32,067 --> 00:02:36,516
error and recovery state are separate checks: a page might show the right message but

32
00:02:36,529 --> 00:02:42,604
leave the customer unable to try again. Keep the action count explicit. Repeated

33
00:02:42,617 --> 00:02:46,504
clicking could generate a different intention or obscure whether the UI recovered

34
00:02:46,516 --> 00:02:51,692
correctly from the controlled first response. The goal is a clear observation of one

35
00:02:51,704 --> 00:02:52,679
error transition.

36
00:02:54,902 --> 00:02:59,377
Query the authoritative account and canonical transfers using the authenticated API

37
00:02:59,390 --> 00:03:05,415
client. Verify the opening balance and an empty collection. This checks that your

38
00:03:05,427 --> 00:03:10,590
mocked browser submission did not create an unexpected backend effect. It also

39
00:03:10,602 --> 00:03:14,752
confirms you did not accidentally leave a real submission path active alongside the

40
00:03:14,765 --> 00:03:20,115
route handler. Explain the causal limit carefully: unchanged state follows because

41
00:03:20,127 --> 00:03:25,115
this request was intercepted before delivery. It does not prove a production service

42
00:03:25,127 --> 00:03:29,152
guarantees rollback whenever a customer receives a five hundred three response.

43
00:03:31,384 --> 00:03:36,222
Remove the route handler when the focused case ends, or let the isolated context close

44
00:03:36,234 --> 00:03:41,009
under the fixture's ownership. If you reuse a page within a larger scenario,

45
00:03:41,196 --> 00:03:46,684
unregister the exact handler before attempting a real transfer. Otherwise, the next

46
00:03:46,696 --> 00:03:50,946
operation may keep receiving the synthetic error and your diagnosis will point at the

47
00:03:50,959 --> 00:03:57,284
wrong service. Prefer a separate fixture for the mocked case. Each test then has a

48
00:03:57,297 --> 00:04:01,534
clearly defined environment and does not depend on cleanup from a previous scenario.

49
00:04:03,762 --> 00:04:09,137
Place the mocked UI case beside the integrated successful transfer. In the first, the

50
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:14,474
test supplies a controlled response and observes browser behavior. In the second, the

51
00:04:14,487 --> 00:04:19,937
browser calls real local services and the test verifies their resulting state. Both

52
00:04:19,950 --> 00:04:25,324
are useful, but they answer different questions. The mock provides a deterministic way

53
00:04:25,337 --> 00:04:30,475
to exercise an error presentation that might be difficult to trigger reliably. The

54
00:04:30,487 --> 00:04:34,962
integrated case checks compatibility across the implemented boundaries for its actual

55
00:04:34,975 --> 00:04:38,100
environment, data, and execution conditions.

56
00:04:40,316 --> 00:04:44,978
Suppose you want to test a genuine service interruption next. Define where the

57
00:04:44,991 --> 00:04:49,616
interruption occurs, what the authoritative system may already have committed, and how

58
00:04:49,628 --> 00:04:54,466
the client recovers the same intention. Use a separate controlled environment and

59
00:04:54,478 --> 00:05:00,016
fault scenario. Do not relabel the existing browser mock as a distributed outage test.

60
00:05:00,878 --> 00:05:05,004
A timeout after commitment can require looking up the original transfer rather than

61
00:05:05,016 --> 00:05:10,566
issuing a new one. Idempotency gives a foundation, but a complete recovery scenario

62
00:05:10,578 --> 00:05:12,891
needs its own contract and observations.

63
00:05:15,118 --> 00:05:20,243
Ask AI to review the route handler with the actual error contract. Require it to

64
00:05:20,256 --> 00:05:25,568
identify the intercepted method, URL, response shape, and assertions about the browser

65
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:31,906
and backend. Then ask which guarantees remain untested. Reject a patch that returns

66
00:05:31,918 --> 00:05:36,830
success from the mock while claiming to validate real transfer settlement. Also

67
00:05:36,843 --> 00:05:42,093
inspect whether the handler remains installed after the case. This review turns AI

68
00:05:42,106 --> 00:05:46,230
into an assistant for checking test scope, rather than a generator of convenient

69
00:05:46,243 --> 00:05:49,156
responses that make every scenario look successful.

70
00:05:51,384 --> 00:05:56,797
Name the result as a UI error-handling test with a browser-supplied response. Include

71
00:05:56,809 --> 00:06:01,272
the synthetic status and the actual observed alert, button state, and authoritative

72
00:06:01,284 --> 00:06:06,672
state checks. Keep its coverage separate from the core successful, rejected, and

73
00:06:06,684 --> 00:06:12,297
idempotent transfer cases. The displayed report labels describe categories, not a

74
00:06:12,309 --> 00:06:18,822
fabricated result. Populate the observation fields only after execution. When another

75
00:06:18,834 --> 00:06:22,822
team reads the regression summary, they should understand immediately that this case

76
00:06:22,834 --> 00:06:27,522
tested presentation and recovery controls without exercising a real service outage.

77
00:06:29,738 --> 00:06:34,263
Your checkpoint is an executed, isolated mock case and a short explanation of its

78
00:06:34,276 --> 00:06:39,663
limits. Demonstrate the alert, the recovered button, and the unchanged local account

79
00:06:39,676 --> 00:06:45,326
state, using actual evidence. Then name one separate integrated failure scenario you

80
00:06:45,338 --> 00:06:50,576
would add if the product required it, such as a lost response after commitment. This

81
00:06:50,588 --> 00:06:54,250
distinction prevents inflated coverage claims while preserving the value of

82
00:06:54,263 --> 00:07:00,363
deterministic UI tests. Next, use traces and a controlled implementation defect to

83
00:07:00,376 --> 00:07:02,938
diagnose failure without weakening assertions.
